1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,585
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,605 --> 00:00:07,615
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,015 --> 00:00:15,395
This is Lecture 8, motion coordination and machine dynamics.

4
00:00:15,795 --> 00:00:19,574
So far we've taken the machine apart one mechanism at a time.

5
00:00:19,594 --> 00:00:22,128
Today we put them back on one main shaft.

6
00:00:22,148 --> 00:00:24,806
What happens in what order within a stitch,

7
00:00:24,826 --> 00:00:27,608
what happens to the others when you move one,

8
00:00:27,628 --> 00:00:30,535
and why the whole machine shakes at high speed.

9
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:34,571
Here's the shop-floor problem.

10
00:00:34,971 --> 00:00:36,652
To meet an order,

11
00:00:36,672 --> 00:00:45,057
a shirt factory wants to take a line of lockstitch machines from 5000 to 6000 r/min.

12
00:00:45,457 --> 00:00:52,303
With this lecture's illustrative masses and a balance ratio of 0.45,

13
00:00:52,323 --> 00:00:58,865
the maximum resultant inertia force at 5000 is about 368 newtons,

14
00:00:58,885 --> 00:01:04,015
and the needle bar at bottom dead centre about 654.

15
00:01:04,415 --> 00:01:12,475
Needle-in-fabric and dog gripping overlap by only 12.5 degrees.

16
00:01:12,875 --> 00:01:18,815
The manual's standard type is rated 5500.

17
00:01:19,215 --> 00:01:23,955
What happens at 6000, and what speed should they choose?

18
00:01:24,355 --> 00:01:25,855
We'll answer at the end.

19
00:01:27,375 --> 00:01:29,635
One shaft, four mechanisms.

20
00:01:31,155 --> 00:01:33,975
Two things happen all the time.

21
00:01:34,375 --> 00:01:36,459
One machine skips on knits;

22
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:41,836
the mechanic retards the hook seven or eight degrees, the skips fall,

23
00:01:41,856 --> 00:01:44,486
and soon the needle thread breaks:

24
00:01:44,506 --> 00:01:50,253
hook timing also decides whether the take-up gives the loop enough thread.

25
00:01:50,653 --> 00:01:56,945
Another machine goes from 4000 to 5000 r/min and the head shakes:

26
00:01:56,965 --> 00:02:00,635
inertia force goes with speed squared,

27
00:02:00,655 --> 00:02:05,296
so 25 percent faster means 1.56 times the force.

28
00:02:05,696 --> 00:02:10,636
On the right, the four mechanisms light up in turn.

29
00:02:11,036 --> 00:02:13,136
They all hang on one shaft.

30
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:18,036
Let's walk through one stitch by shaft angle.

31
00:02:18,436 --> 00:02:22,736
At 0 degrees the needle bar is at top dead centre.

32
00:02:23,136 --> 00:02:29,757
At 55 the take-up is tightest and the stitch is set.

33
00:02:30,157 --> 00:02:39,259
At 101.7 the needle enters; at 114.2 the feed dog drops;

34
00:02:39,279 --> 00:02:43,495
180 is bottom dead centre.

35
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:51,466
After about 2 millimetres of rise, the hook catches the loop at 206.

36
00:02:51,866 --> 00:02:57,570
The needle leaves at 258.3.

37
00:02:57,970 --> 00:03:04,091
The take-up gives out the most thread at 293.

38
00:03:04,491 --> 00:03:09,656
The dog rises and starts feeding at 325.8.

39
00:03:10,056 --> 00:03:15,959
Around 341 the loop casts off, the take-up pulls it tight,

40
00:03:15,979 --> 00:03:18,716
and the next stitch begins.

41
00:03:20,236 --> 00:03:22,635
Draw one stitch as a disc.

42
00:03:23,035 --> 00:03:29,322
From the outside in: needle in the fabric, hook carrying the loop,

43
00:03:29,342 --> 00:03:33,335
take-up pulling thread, feed dog gripping.

44
00:03:33,735 --> 00:03:34,955
Notice three things.

45
00:03:35,355 --> 00:03:40,362
First, needle-in and dog-gripping almost meet end to end,

46
00:03:40,382 --> 00:03:46,359
overlapping only 12.5 degrees, just 0.42 milliseconds at 5000 r/min,

47
00:03:46,379 --> 00:03:51,650
and by then the dog's horizontal motion is essentially done.

48
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:59,652
Second, the hook's 135 degrees with the loop span the needle's exit.

49
00:04:00,052 --> 00:04:06,284
Third, when the take-up is tightest at 55, the dog is still feeding.

50
00:04:07,804 --> 00:04:12,864
Cross-mechanism constraints and phase windows.

51
00:04:14,384 --> 00:04:17,861
Each earlier lecture had its own criteria;

52
00:04:17,881 --> 00:04:22,524
together they form a set of cross-mechanism constraints.

53
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:29,628
Hook: the needle rises 1.6 to 2.5 millimetres at the catch,

54
00:04:29,648 --> 00:04:34,984
which is 203.2 to 209.2 degrees of shaft angle.

55
00:04:35,384 --> 00:04:36,006
Take-up:

56
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:42,663
slack before the eye enters must stay within the 10-millimetre check-spring stroke,

57
00:04:42,683 --> 00:04:46,353
the shortfall after entry within 1 millimetre,

58
00:04:46,373 --> 00:04:50,364
and catch to cast-off needs a 1-millimetre margin.

59
00:04:50,764 --> 00:04:58,824
Feed: the fabric may move no more than 0.05 millimetres with the needle in.

60
00:04:59,224 --> 00:05:04,481
And the catch also decides when the loop starts taking thread,

61
00:05:04,501 --> 00:05:07,204
so hook and take-up are coupled.

62
00:05:08,724 --> 00:05:13,544
This is Virtual Lab 8-1, the whole-machine timing editor.

63
00:05:13,944 --> 00:05:18,535
The hook timing is swinging back and forth between plus and minus 9 degrees.

64
00:05:18,935 --> 00:05:24,700
On the left, the catch band slides along the timing tracks; on the right,

65
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:27,395
the six criteria are checked live.

66
00:05:27,795 --> 00:05:33,447
Watch which one turns red first: it's the needle rise at the catch,

67
00:05:33,467 --> 00:05:34,547
at both ends.

68
00:05:34,947 --> 00:05:38,049
The take-up margin changes too,

69
00:05:38,069 --> 00:05:43,790
losing about 0.1 to 0.2 millimetres per degree of retard,

70
00:05:43,810 --> 00:05:46,307
but it isn't used up yet.

71
00:05:47,827 --> 00:05:52,525
Move one mechanism at a time, keep the others at standard,

72
00:05:52,545 --> 00:05:55,047
and you get the usable windows.

73
00:05:55,447 --> 00:06:00,393
The hook: about minus 2.8 to plus 3.2 degrees,

74
00:06:00,413 --> 00:06:04,387
both ends limited by the needle rise.

75
00:06:04,787 --> 00:06:11,105
The take-up: about minus 3 to plus 6, for different reasons at each end.

76
00:06:11,505 --> 00:06:18,385
Advance it and the slack before entry exceeds the 10 millimetres the check

77
00:06:18,405 --> 00:06:19,877
spring can hold;

78
00:06:19,897 --> 00:06:25,565
retard it and the shortfall after entry exceeds 1 millimetre.

79
00:06:25,965 --> 00:06:31,481
The feed is the most lopsided: only 1 degree of retard,

80
00:06:31,501 --> 00:06:34,501
but up to minus 47 of advance.

81
00:06:36,021 --> 00:06:40,881
The windows also tell us the order for fixing phases in design.

82
00:06:41,281 --> 00:06:45,581
First the needle bar, the reference for all timing.

83
00:06:45,981 --> 00:06:49,220
Then the catch, from the needle rise.

84
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:55,080
Then the take-up, from the catch and the loop's thread consumption.

85
00:06:55,480 --> 00:07:01,375
Last, the feed, fitted into the gap while the needle is out of the fabric.

86
00:07:01,775 --> 00:07:07,151
Do it in the reverse order and what you fixed earlier has to be redone.

87
00:07:08,671 --> 00:07:12,091
Machine inertia forces and the counterweight.

88
00:07:13,611 --> 00:07:18,486
Opposite the needle-bar crank sits a counterweight.

89
00:07:18,886 --> 00:07:23,758
Its centrifugal force has the same frequency as the crank pin's,

90
00:07:23,778 --> 00:07:25,746
in the opposite direction.

91
00:07:26,146 --> 00:07:33,512
We write its size as the rotating mass plus k times the reciprocating mass;

92
00:07:33,532 --> 00:07:35,778
k is the balance ratio.

93
00:07:36,178 --> 00:07:41,478
Here k sweeps back and forth between 0 and 1.

94
00:07:41,878 --> 00:07:48,382
With small k the locus is stretched vertically; with large k, horizontally.

95
00:07:48,782 --> 00:07:52,571
Somewhere in between it's closest to a circle,

96
00:07:52,591 --> 00:07:57,042
and the maximum resultant at the top left is smallest.

97
00:07:58,562 --> 00:08:04,942
The numbers at 5000 r/min.

98
00:08:05,342 --> 00:08:10,838
With k equal to 0, balancing only the rotating part,

99
00:08:10,858 --> 00:08:17,202
the maximum resultant is about 582 newtons, mostly vertical.

100
00:08:17,602 --> 00:08:24,798
With k equal to 1, the first-order reciprocating force is cancelled vertically,

101
00:08:24,818 --> 00:08:29,822
but an equal new force appears horizontally: about 609.

102
00:08:30,222 --> 00:08:37,884
With k at 0.45 it drops to about 368.

103
00:08:38,284 --> 00:08:44,281
Needle bar and crank alone would give a best k of about 0.6;

104
00:08:44,301 --> 00:08:49,595
the take-up's first-order component leans horizontal,

105
00:08:49,615 --> 00:08:53,104
which brings it down to about 0.45.

106
00:08:54,624 --> 00:09:02,331
Split the resultant at k equal to 0.45 into harmonics.

107
00:09:02,731 --> 00:09:10,232
Vertically, the first order is about 234 newtons and the second about 119;

108
00:09:10,252 --> 00:09:14,603
horizontally, the first order is about 338;

109
00:09:14,623 --> 00:09:20,193
the third and fourth are a few tens of newtons or less.

110
00:09:20,593 --> 00:09:25,744
The vertical second order comes mainly from the needle bar,

111
00:09:25,764 --> 00:09:29,337
the lambda cos 2 phi term from Lecture 3.

112
00:09:29,737 --> 00:09:32,747
It runs at twice shaft frequency,

113
00:09:32,767 --> 00:09:37,982
and a counterweight on the crank can do nothing about it.

114
00:09:38,382 --> 00:09:43,282
That's why a counterweight never removes the vibration completely.

115
00:09:44,802 --> 00:09:50,035
A robot loading and unloading a press works like the feed:

116
00:09:50,055 --> 00:09:56,738
it may reach in only during the phase when the ram is up and the die open,

117
00:09:56,758 --> 00:10:00,542
and must be out before the ram comes down.

118
00:10:00,942 --> 00:10:04,762
That's the counterpart of feeding only while the needle is out.

119
00:10:05,162 --> 00:10:06,492
In control terms,

120
00:10:06,512 --> 00:10:11,576
the robot treats the press crankshaft encoder as its main shaft,

121
00:10:11,596 --> 00:10:16,422
follows it with an electronic cam and sets interlock windows.

122
00:10:16,822 --> 00:10:23,364
Raise the press from 15 to 18 strokes a minute with the same path,

123
00:10:23,384 --> 00:10:30,722
and accelerations and inertia forces grow 1.44 times: the same square law.

124
00:10:32,242 --> 00:10:33,941
You see the same thing at home.

125
00:10:34,341 --> 00:10:37,273
When the laundry bunches to one side,

126
00:10:37,293 --> 00:10:43,894
the washing machine shakes hard during the spin and can even walk across the floor:

127
00:10:43,914 --> 00:10:48,521
the unbalanced centrifugal force grows with speed squared.

128
00:10:48,921 --> 00:10:53,666
So before spinning, it turns slowly a few times to spread the load,

129
00:10:53,686 --> 00:10:58,288
which is like choosing the balance; and when it senses imbalance,

130
00:10:58,308 --> 00:10:59,781
it slows itself down.

131
00:11:00,181 --> 00:11:06,353
Same idea as choosing a balance ratio and staying within the rated speed of a

132
00:11:06,373 --> 00:11:07,881
lockstitch machine.

133
00:11:09,401 --> 00:11:10,381
Back to the start.

134
00:11:10,781 --> 00:11:14,493
At 6000, multiply by 1.44:

135
00:11:14,513 --> 00:11:25,403
the maximum resultant becomes about 530 newtons and the needle bar at bottom

136
00:11:25,423 --> 00:11:28,561
dead centre about 942.

137
00:11:28,961 --> 00:11:37,629
The 12.5-degree overlap shrinks from 0.42 to 0.35 milliseconds,

138
00:11:37,649 --> 00:11:43,421
so the timing margins get tighter in time.

139
00:11:43,821 --> 00:11:49,111
And 6000 is above the manual's 5500 for the standard type,

140
00:11:49,131 --> 00:11:52,041
so it shouldn't be run for long.

141
00:11:52,441 --> 00:11:58,159
Keep to 5500 or less: multiply by 1.21,

142
00:11:58,179 --> 00:12:07,134
giving about 445 newtons resultant and 791 on the needle bar.

143
00:12:07,534 --> 00:12:13,474
Raise output by cutting downtime and balancing the line instead.

144
00:12:14,994 --> 00:12:18,756
Now Virtual lab 8-1, the whole-machine timing editor.

145
00:12:18,776 --> 00:12:23,752
On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself. The criterion checks,

146
00:12:23,772 --> 00:12:27,963
the task checks and the maximum resultant are blurred here.

147
00:12:27,983 --> 00:12:30,746
Read them when you do the lab yourself.

148
00:12:31,146 --> 00:12:35,444
First record the six criteria at standard.

149
00:12:35,464 --> 00:12:42,126
Then drag only the hook timing and see which one turns red first.

150
00:12:42,526 --> 00:12:45,329
Reset, and drag only the take-up phase.

151
00:12:45,349 --> 00:12:49,673
Find the earliest and latest phases where everything passes,

152
00:12:49,693 --> 00:12:52,786
note which criterion turns red at each end,

153
00:12:52,806 --> 00:12:55,826
and enter them in the window box to check.

154
00:12:56,226 --> 00:12:59,852
Task one. Press Scramble three phases.

155
00:12:59,872 --> 00:13:06,472
See which criteria turn red and on which tracks, then bring the hook,

156
00:13:06,492 --> 00:13:09,926
take-up and feed back one at a time.

157
00:13:10,326 --> 00:13:14,784
Next, the machine's inertia forces. On the left,

158
00:13:14,804 --> 00:13:19,170
the locus of the resultant force; on the right,

159
00:13:19,190 --> 00:13:22,342
its vertical and horizontal parts.

160
00:13:22,742 --> 00:13:28,717
At 5000 r/min, drag the balance ratio from 0 to 1,

161
00:13:28,737 --> 00:13:33,513
record the maximum resultant every 0.15,

162
00:13:33,533 --> 00:13:38,908
and watch the locus change from tall to wide.

163
00:13:39,308 --> 00:13:47,808
Task three. Go above 6000 r/min and choose a balance ratio yourself,

164
00:13:47,828 --> 00:13:57,706
so the maximum resultant is no more than 550 newtons with all six criteria met.

165
00:13:58,106 --> 00:14:01,926
The guide and the report template are on the platform.

166
00:14:03,446 --> 00:14:09,123
Virtual lab 8-2 is machine diagnosis, and it brings Lectures 4 to 8 together.

167
00:14:09,143 --> 00:14:14,228
The page gives you a faulty lockstitch machine with a machine number,

168
00:14:14,248 --> 00:14:16,965
but not the cause. The stitch counts,

169
00:14:16,985 --> 00:14:20,812
the gauge readings and the log are blurred here too;

170
00:14:20,832 --> 00:14:23,106
the symptoms are yours to read.

171
00:14:23,506 --> 00:14:28,375
First sew 30 stitches, and look at the top, the underside,

172
00:14:28,395 --> 00:14:30,735
and how the machine behaves.

173
00:14:31,135 --> 00:14:35,020
From the symptoms, list two or three possible causes,

174
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:39,515
each with one measurement, and measure the most likely first.

175
00:14:39,915 --> 00:14:45,918
Compare each reading with standard. Adjust only the setting that's off,

176
00:14:45,938 --> 00:14:49,990
one thing at a time, then measure and sew again.

177
00:14:50,390 --> 00:14:53,649
Keep going until all 30 stitches are good,

178
00:14:53,669 --> 00:14:57,005
without disturbing anything that was right.

179
00:14:57,025 --> 00:15:02,781
One symptom often has several causes, and only measuring tells them apart.

180
00:15:02,801 --> 00:15:06,060
That's how you set up a real machine, too.

181
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:12,548
The numbers you set when adjusting a machine have to be recorded,

182
00:15:12,568 --> 00:15:18,149
machine by machine, in the factory. This is the WenQuest digital factory.

183
00:15:18,169 --> 00:15:23,367
The adjustment station measures the hook clearance on every machine,

184
00:15:23,387 --> 00:15:27,203
the readings go over the bus into a control chart,

185
00:15:27,223 --> 00:15:31,040
and if the data drifts the system raises an alarm.

186
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:36,836
Switch to the trace page. On the right is the machine record:

187
00:15:36,856 --> 00:15:43,051
each machine's hook batch, hook clearance, hook timing and final test,

188
00:15:43,071 --> 00:15:46,780
each one a record in the history database.

189
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:51,027
Drag the serial number and look at them one by one.

190
00:15:51,047 --> 00:15:56,485
The hook timing and clearance from earlier lectures are set, logged and,

191
00:15:56,505 --> 00:16:01,413
if something goes wrong, traced like this, one machine at a time.

192
00:16:01,433 --> 00:16:05,280
We'll walk through the whole factory in Lecture 27.

193
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:12,069
The assignment is a machine diagnosis report, with four questions. One,

194
00:16:12,089 --> 00:16:13,485
in Virtual lab 8-2,

195
00:16:13,505 --> 00:16:19,370
use the machine number your teacher gives you with 3 faults and clear them all,

196
00:16:19,390 --> 00:16:24,287
in no more than 8 test seams and without disturbing anything else.

197
00:16:24,307 --> 00:16:29,800
For each fault, write the symptom, the causes you suspected, the readings,

198
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:33,376
your diagnosis, the adjustment and the re-check.

199
00:16:33,776 --> 00:16:39,165
Two, which two faults on your machine looked most alike,

200
00:16:39,185 --> 00:16:42,836
and which measurement told them apart?

201
00:16:43,236 --> 00:16:45,872
Three, retard the hook by 4 degrees,

202
00:16:45,892 --> 00:16:50,004
use the Lecture 3 formula to find the rise at the catch,

203
00:16:50,024 --> 00:16:52,366
say which criterion fails first,

204
00:16:52,386 --> 00:16:57,531
and explain why the mechanic at the start began breaking thread. Four,

205
00:16:57,551 --> 00:17:02,401
explain why a counterweight can't remove the vibration completely,

206
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:06,829
in terms of first and second order, vertical and horizontal,

207
00:17:06,849 --> 00:17:11,182
and the take-up lever, and suggest two design improvements.

208
00:17:11,582 --> 00:17:17,517
Make the report one PDF and submit it on the WenQuest platform.

209
00:17:19,037 --> 00:17:23,770
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

210
00:17:23,790 --> 00:17:31,314
needle-in-fabric and dog gripping overlap by 12.5 degrees. At 4000 r/min,

211
00:17:31,334 --> 00:17:35,137
how many milliseconds does that last?

212
00:17:35,537 --> 00:17:42,547
Or, when the take-up is tightest at 55 degrees, what is the feed dog doing?

213
00:17:42,947 --> 00:17:48,349
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

214
00:17:49,869 --> 00:17:51,729
Three things to remember.

215
00:17:52,129 --> 00:17:56,101
One: all the mechanisms hang on one main shaft,

216
00:17:56,121 --> 00:17:58,988
so whenever you change one timing,

217
00:17:59,008 --> 00:18:04,509
go back to the whole-machine timing diagram and check the others.

218
00:18:04,909 --> 00:18:12,322
Two: the design order for phases is needle bar, catch, take-up, feed.

219
00:18:12,722 --> 00:18:17,133
Three: inertia force goes with speed squared;

220
00:18:17,153 --> 00:18:20,678
a balance ratio around 0.45 is best,

221
00:18:20,698 --> 00:18:27,373
but the vertical second order can't be removed by the counterweight.

222
00:18:27,773 --> 00:18:32,362
The assignment is the machine diagnosis report;

223
00:18:32,382 --> 00:18:36,186
the quiz and both labs are on WenQuest.

224
00:18:36,586 --> 00:18:40,766
The slides, the virtual labs, the 3D prototype,

225
00:18:40,786 --> 00:18:47,020
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

226
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:53,006
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

227
00:18:53,406 --> 00:18:58,886
That completes the lockstitch machine. Next time, a different machine,

228
00:18:58,906 --> 00:19:00,536
the overlock machine.

229
00:19:00,556 --> 00:19:06,900
We'll see how three threads and two loopers wrap the edge while a knife trims it,

230
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:11,536
and how two feed dogs give differential feed. See you then.
